setline(91, "* output (stages H and I): in stage H the program converts `nk1`, `rho1`, `nk2`, `rho2`, the digest and the centre out of the Montgomery representation (6 charged multiplications) and encodes each as 64 hexadecimal digits in `enc_hex` of `single.c`, 12 operations per digit (loop update, comparison and branch; `pos = 63 - i`; word index; mask; scaling of the shift count; shift; mask; table load; byte store) and 5 more per call. In stage I the program charges, before it formats its report and before it prints the one total, everything it executes after the last algorithm step; this is inside the total `N`: (i) the formatting and copying of the whole report: every report line, the collision lines included, is formatted by the small `printf` of `nolibc.c` (`vfmt`, a loop over the format string with one digit loop per number and one copy loop per field) into one static buffer of `OUTCAP` = 8,192 bytes; a byte of output costs at most about 40 operations (format scan, the division and remainder of a digit, the padding and copy loops with a comparison, a store and an increment) and a conversion adds at most about 150 and yields at least one byte, so `FMT_PER_BYTE` = 256 per byte is an upper bound, and the buffer is charged in full, `256 * 8,192` = 2,097,152 operations, whatever it holds (the program ends with exit status 3, and no collision line, if the buffer overflowed, which the check shows did not happen); (ii) the loops of the report over the stage and trial rows, the sums, and the 30-term series of `log2`: `REPORT_LOOPS` = 20,000; (iii) the process entry (`_start`, `c_start`, under 20 instructions) and the buffer-length check: 100; (iv) the single `write` system call that emits the whole buffer: `SYS_WRITE` = 10,000 + 8 * 8,192 = 75,536 operations (the instruction work in the process is about 10 instructions; the rest stands for the kernel's copy of the buffer and its own instructions); (v) the single `exit_group` system call: `SYS_EXIT` = 10,000; (vi) the bookkeeping of the stages (reading the nine counters, the subtractions and additions into the stage row, the search of the row by name, the reset of the memory peak): `STAGE_BK` = 1,000 per stage opened (a bound: under 400 operations), plus 1,000 for the closing of stage I; (vii) the statements of `main` before the counters start (`rng_seed`, `poly_degree_m`, the argument test): `PROLOGUE` = 500 (a bound), and the four snapshots of the counters in `powx_lean` for the powering breakdown line: 500 (a bound). The write is made once, as the last action, so the total printed in the buffer is the total of the counters with all of (i) to (vii) already added. There is no other system call in the run: the program contains no `mmap`, `munmap`, `clock_gettime` or second `write`.")
setline(92, "* allocator: `xalloc` and `xfree` (`common.c`) serve every buffer from one static arena in the bss (no system call), with a 64-byte header per block and a first-fit search over the blocks in address order; blocks are split when the remainder is at least 128 bytes and merged with free neighbours on `xfree`. Every executed load, store, comparison, branch and address or size computation of both functions is charged where it is written in `common.c`, with the rules of this section (`ldz` and `stz` charge a load or store and its address operation; each comparison and its branch is charged 2; each add, subtract or mask 1; each loop pass of the block search 3), and the statistics lines (bytes in use, live blocks, peaks, extent) are charged a flat `ALLOC_STATS` = 64 per call (a bound: about 40). The allocator charges are inside the totals of the stages in which the calls occur. The memory it uses is the arena of section 7.")
